# V. Risk factors

# 5.1 AI Subversive Risk
AI may fundamentally change the way academic research, legal research, and risk assessment work. If the AI model can generate analytical conclusions directly from the raw data, demand for the ARLX intermediate analysis tool may decline. This is the central reason for the 50% decline in share prices.

#5.2 Open access to sport risks
The open access campaign in the academic press continues to develop, and government and funding agencies require public funding for research results to be made freely available.

#5.3 Data privacy regulations tightened
Risk analysis operations rely on a large number of personal data (including public records and government sources). The tightening of global data privacy legislation may limit data collection and use, directly affecting the effectiveness and availability of the product of the Risk plate (approximately 37 per cent of revenue).

#5.4 Risk of exchange rate fluctuations
RELLX reports in pounds sterling but most of its income is denominated in United States dollars and other currencies, and exchange rate fluctuations have a significant impact on reporting performance. EPS grew by 7 per cent in 2025, compared to 10 per cent at the fixed exchange rate.

#5.5 Vulnerability of exhibition operations
The exhibition business (about 14% of the income) suffered a severe damage during the epidemic. Although it has been restored, it is the most cyclical plate with mixed patterns of activity and economic cycles.

#5.6 Uncertainty of the high valuation regression
Even if the basics are good, the market's valuation of the information services plate may be priced for longer periods. Investors' emotional recovery times and catalysts are uncertain.

